                <pb facs="0905" n="823" sameAs="frus:frus1865p3/pg823"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Bigelow to Mr.
                        Seward</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#float-left">No. 272.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States</hi>,
                                <hi rendition="#italic">Paris</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1866-02-15"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 15, 1866.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I learn from an unofficial source that
                    Gregorio Barandiran, the diplomatic representative of the Archduke Maximilian at
                    Vienna, formerly secretary of legation under Señor Robles at Washington, is now
                    at Paris, for money to fit out 10,000 Austrians, which, he says, are ready to
                    embark from Trieste for Mexico. The Mexican commissioner informed him that there
                    was no money in his hands. I am not sure of learning the result of the
                    minister’s suit here, as the money, if furnished, must come through indirect and
                    concealed channels.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I am, sir, with great respect, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>JOHN BIGELOW.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, &amp;c., &amp;c.,
                        &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
